      "sha256": "c7ce956f09e77e5c15e19ac6e96dbca74f385cb24d3f767893dab617fde37ae1",
      "text": "<!-- PDF_PAGE: 1 -->\n## PDF page 1 — no printed page number\n\n[supplied: Masthead across the full width; three columns below. Reading order: masthead, left column, middle column, right column.]\n\n# 𝔗𝔥𝔢 𝔈𝔵𝔞𝔪𝔭𝔩𝔢 𝔊𝔞𝔷𝔢𝔱𝔱𝔢\n\n*A Weekly Record of Town and Country*\n\nVOL. IV. — NO. 1. — WHOLE NO. 157. — ANYTOWN, SATURDAY, MAY 1, 1875. — PRICE TEN CENTS.\n\n### TO OUR READERS\n\nWith this number the *Gazette* enters upon its fourth volume, and upon a new plan: it will appear once a fortnight instead of once a week, in eight pages instead of four. Our motto, 𝔉𝔦𝔡𝔢𝔩𝔦𝔱𝔞𝔰, stands where it stood.\n\nWe thank the many readers who wrote to us during the winter. Their letters are printed, as space allows, on page 4.\n\n*(continued on page 2)*\n\n### A CORRECTION\n\nIn our last number the meeting of the Improvement Society was given as Tuesday. It is to be held on Thursday, at the Town Hall, at eight o’clock.\n\n---\n\n### AROUND THE COUNTY\n\nNotes from our correspondents in the townships. Contributions should reach the office by the Monday before publication.\n\n#### Eastfield\n\nThe Reading Circle met on the 20th at the schoolhouse. Officers were chosen for the year, and the library committee reported forty-two volumes on the shelves. — JANE DOE.\n\n*(Around the County continued on page 2.)*\n\n---\n\n“Let every man be swift to hear.”\n\n— *From an old almanac.*\n\n",
